<PageHero eyebrow="Resource" title="How Long Do You Have to Challenge a Dismissal in Spain?." description="The Workers’ Statute provides a general 20-working-day limitation period for dismissal actions. The exact calculation and any interruption must be checked against the circumstances." primaryLabel="Contact the firm" primaryHref="/en/contact" secondaryLabel="More resources" secondaryHref="/en/resources" note="General information · Check the current official source before acting" />
<section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>The date you were notified matters</h2><p>A dismissal action is subject to a short statutory period. For the general rule, Article 59 of the Workers’ Statute provides 20 working days. The precise calculation depends on the dates and procedural circumstances of the case.</p><h2>Why you should not work from an estimate</h2><p>Start with the exact notification date and preserve the letter or evidence of communication. A mistake about the date can change the available time to act.</p><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2015-11430#a59" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Article 59 at the BOE →</a></p><div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong> general information cannot calculate your individual deadline. Where a dismissal has already been notified, a specific review should be made promptly.</div></div></section>
<CTA title="Need to check your specific situation?" text="Tell us your dates, status and what you are trying to achieve." label="Contact the firm" />
